    "alert_id": "EX2_001",
    "title": "TDF nephrotoxicity vs acute gastroenteritis AKI",
    "message": "Guidelines list acute kidney injury as a major adverse event of TDF and recommend LFTs and RFTs when deranged. Patient has severe creatinine rise (0.9 to 2.4) after 5 days of watery diarrhoea and vomiting with dehydration. Evidence for drug attribution: TDF is nephrotoxic and can cause AKI. Evidence against: the presentation is acute over days with household contacts sharing the illness, urea:creatinine ratio 24:1 suggests prerenal dehydration, and urinalysis shows no protein or glucose (TDF toxicity typically causes proteinuria/glycosuria). The AKI is more likely prerenal from gastroenteritis than TDF toxicity.",
    "issue_type": "toxicity",
    "severity": "high",
    "recommended_action": "Resuscitate with IV fluids first and reassess renal function before deciding to stop TDF permanently. If creatinine recovers, TDF may be safely continued.",
